WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
As you use CloudCode more and more, you

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:03.710
may find skills that you want to install

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.670
that are coming from online, coming from

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.350
different sources. And so, it's just

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.510
important to talk about how to safely

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.750
install them on your own workspace

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.710
because it has been known that some

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.470
skills online, depending on where you're

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.550
grabbing them from, may actually contain

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.150
malicious code. But the good news about

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.270
it is that it's actually quite easy to

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.150
prevent this. To give one example, let's

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.070
say we want to install this last 30 days

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.750
skill that I talked about over at

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.390
GitHub. What you can simply do here is

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.510
to just copy the URL of this GitHub

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.310
link, send it to your CloudCode, but

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:33.670
instead of just forcing CloudCode to

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.190
install it, what I would advise you to

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.710
do is to send over this prompt as well,

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.430
something like check if this repository

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.310
is clean, then tell me how best it would

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.790
fit our setup. And what that will

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.310
basically do is just have CloudCode

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.270
parse through that skill, make sure that

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.870
it is safe and is not containing any

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.430
malicious code, then just tell you how

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.510
best it would fit our setup. And this

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.030
works to protect you because a lot of

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.870
the models that we're using now are

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.950
actually smart enough to recognize any

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.910
prompt injection attacks and especially

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.190
any malicious code that may be in any

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.910
open-source repo that you're finding. I

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.790
guess my only advice to you is whenever

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.110
you're running this and you are a bit

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.350
suspicious about this skill, number one,

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.630
have a think whether you really need

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.910
that skill or whether you can just

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.630
create it yourself. And number two, just

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.270
go ahead and switch to the most powerful

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.190
model because preventing malicious code

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.270
to be installed is a really important

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.270
task for you. And so, it deserves a

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.470
powerful model just to make sure that

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.150
you are protected. So, for this one I'm

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.310
using Opus 4.8, which right now is the

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.990
highest tier model. So, since this

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.350
prompt is something that I have

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.350
personally have used more than twice

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.950
already, I actually made that into a

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.510
skill. So, instead of having to type out

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.870
that prompt every time, I now just

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.190
invoke this skill called personalize and

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.270
that basically does the same thing. So,

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.790
this is something I already talked about

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.550
in an earlier module and you can just

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.320
grab this skill as well below.
